course: english
lesson: 8
title: Endings that sound like «shun»: -tion, -sion, -ssion, -cian
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Liam writes «stashun» for «station». It sounds right, but in English the /shun/ ending is almost never spelled «shun». It has four main spellings, -tion, -sion, -ssion and -cian, and the commonest by far is -tion: station, action, question, information.
explain: The secret is the root word, the word you start from. Root ends in t or te: -tion (act, action; invent, invention; complete, completion). Root ends in ss: -ssion (discuss, discussion; express, expression). Root ends in d, de, se or nd: usually -sion (comprehend, comprehension; expand, expansion; tense, tension). Root ends in c and the word is a person's job: -cian (music, musician; magic, magician; electric, electrician).
explain: Listen too. When the ending sounds softer, /zhun/ instead of /shun/, it is nearly always -sion: television, division, decision, explosion. Nadia in London grumbles that «television» sounds like «zhun»; she is right, and that sound is her clue.
explain: Adding -tion often turns a doing word (verb) into a thing word (noun): inform becomes information, educate becomes education. Many of these are school and work words, which is why they feel formal; a clear short word is often better than a long -tion one. And adverts sometimes spell words wrong on purpose to look fun («competishun»), so a sign is no proof of a spelling.
explain: Some words are traps on their own. «Occasion» has two c's and only one s; «musician» keeps the c of «music». When stuck, ask: what is the root, how does it end, and is it a person?
fact: The /shun/ ending is spelled -tion, -sion, -ssion or -cian, almost never «shun».
fact: -tion is the most common spelling: station, action, question.
fact: Roots ending in t or te take -tion: act, action; invent, invention; complete, completion.
fact: Roots ending in ss take -ssion: discuss, discussion; express, expression.
fact: Roots ending in d, de, se or nd usually take -sion: comprehend, comprehension; expand, expansion; tense, tension.
fact: -cian is used for people's jobs from words ending in c: musician, magician, electrician.
fact: The softer /zhun/ sound is usually spelled -sion: television, division, decision.
fact: Adding -tion often turns a verb into a noun: inform, information.
fact: «Occasion» has two c's and one s.
fact: Signs and adverts sometimes misspell words on purpose for effect; that does not make the spelling correct.
fact: Many -tion words sound formal; use them when they are clearer, not just to sound clever.
fact: Slang in this lesson: «bruv» means brother or mate and «peak» means bad luck or annoying (London); «grand» means fine and «yer man» means «that fella» (Ireland); «it's giving» means «it seems like» (Gen Alpha and Gen Z).
exercise: Liam writes «stashun». How is it spelled? | station | stasion | stassion | statian
exercise: Which /shun/ ending is the most common? | -tion | -sion | -ssion | -cian
exercise: Sophie asks why «musician» ends in -cian. What is the reason? | It is a person's job from «music», which ends in c | The word was borrowed from French without any change | Every musical word must end in -cian | -cian is the ending for all long words
exercise: Ryan writes «discution». What is right, and why? | discussion, because «discuss» ends in ss | discusion, because the sound is soft | discution, because -tion is the most common | discussian, because it is about people
exercise: Nadia says «television» ends in /zhun/. Which spelling goes with that sound? | -sion | -tion | -ssion | -cian
exercise: What does «peak» mean in London slang? | Bad luck, annoying | The top of a mountain | Brilliant | Quiet
exercise: Ben writes «comprehention». What is right? | comprehension | comprehenssion | comprehencian | comprehention
exercise: Lucy spots «action» in «act». What does that tell her about «invention»? | Root ends in t, so add -ion: -tion | It must be -sion, because invent is long | It must be -cian, because inventors are people with jobs | Nothing, every -tion word is learned on its own
exercise: Uncle in Dublin says an ad wrote «competishun». Why might it? | To look fun on purpose | Because that is the Irish spelling | Because -tion is old-fashioned now | By law, adverts spell as they sound
exercise: What does «grand» mean in Ireland? | Fine | Huge | Grandmother | Rich
exercise: Jacob writes «occassion». What is right? | occasion | ocassion | occasian | ocasion
exercise: What kind of word does -tion usually make from «inform»? | A noun, a naming word: information | A verb, a doing word: informing | An adjective, a describing word: informal | An adverb, a how word: informally
exercise: Marcus says -tion words are «giving homework vibes». What does «it's giving» mean? | It seems like | It is a present | It is giving up | It is boring
exercise: Which job ends in -cian? | electrician | electrition | musition | magission
exercise: Which root takes -ssion? | express | act | expand | magic
exercise: Why do many -tion words feel formal? | They are common in school and work language | They are always longer than ten letters | They are only ever used by teachers and head teachers | They come from ancient Egyptian
test: «bruv, just write it how it sounds: televishun, stashun, mushun». What is the problem? | The /shun/ ending is almost never spelled «shun» | Nothing, sounding a word out always gives you its spelling | Those are the American spellings | Only «mushun» is wrong; the others are fine
test: «I'm a magition when I grow up». Which spelling is right, and why? | magician: a job from «magic», which ends in c | magition: -tion is the most common ending | magission: magic tricks need ss | magision: the sound is soft like «television»
test: «yer man's sign says competishun, so that's how ya spell it». What is true? | Ads can misspell on purpose; it's «competition» | Signs are always checked by an editor, so it must be right | It is the Irish spelling of the word | Both spellings are fine in school work
test: What would happen if you used -tion for every /shun/ word? | You'd misspell words like «discussion» and «musician» | You would always be right | You'd only misspell the very long words | Teachers would accept it as long as the word sounds right when read aloud
test: «expand, so it's expantion, innit». What is the right spelling and the clue? | expansion: the root ends in nd | expantion: the root ends in t | expanssion: the root ends in ss | expancian: it is a job
test: «-tion words are giving homework vibes, so I'll stuff 'em in my story to sound smart». Good idea? | No, use them when they are clearer, not to show off | Yes, long words always make writing better | Yes, teachers give an extra mark for every -tion word in a story | No, -tion words are banned in stories
test: A cartoon wizard spells his job «wizardcian». Why doesn't -cian fit? | «Wizard» doesn't end in c | -cian is only for women's jobs | -cian is only for words from music | It does fit; every job ends in -cian
test: Mum tests «discuss» → «discussion». Which other pair follows the same pattern? | express, expression | invent, invention | music, musician | divide, division
test: «decision ends in zhun so it's decizhun». Spot the slip. | The soft /zhun/ sound is spelled -sion | It's right; always spell it exactly like it sounds | It should be «decition» | It should be «decissian»
test: ★★★ Which word has the SAME ending spelling as «musician»? | politician | position | passion | television
test: ★★★ «Complete» becomes «completion». What happened to the e? | It dropped, because the ending starts with a vowel | It moved to the front of the word, before the c | It doubled to make «completeetion» | It stayed: «completetion»
test: ★★★ Which list has all four /shun/ spellings, one each? | station, vision, mission, optician | action, fiction, mission, magician | passion, session, mission, tension | nation, motion, potion, lotion
exam: Identify the principal means by which the correct spelling of the ending pronounced /shun/ may be determined. | By reference to the ending of the root word from which the word is formed | By reference to the length of the word in letters and the number of its syllables | By reference to the country in which the word is written | By reference to the first letter of the word
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the ending -cian? | It denotes a person's occupation and is formed from a root terminating in c | It denotes an action or a process and is invariably formed from a root terminating in the letter t | It is employed in all words of more than three syllables | It is an obsolete spelling no longer in use
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning words which terminate in a softer /zhun/ sound. | Such words are commonly spelled with the ending -sion | Such words are invariably spelled with the ending -tion | Such words are spelled with the ending -shun in formal writing | Such words have no settled spelling
exam: It is given that the word «competishun» appears in a commercial advertisement. Ascertain the inference which may properly be drawn. | The misspelling may be deliberate and does not establish the correct form | The spelling is thereby established as correct | The advertisement must be withdrawn by law | The word has changed its spelling in recent years
